        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I'll think about it a bit more, but I'd personally much rather find some Git-native solution to this problem. Maybe there is a way of setting `GIT_DIR` like information per working directory (instead of a single environment variable)?\r\n\r\nSure enough, there is. There can be '.git' *file* at the root of the working tree ('~' in your case) with the following content:\r\n```\r\ngitdir: dotfiles\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis should treat '\\~' as a repository with '\\~/dotfiles' as its '.git' repo.\r\n\r\nHowever, every call to `git` must also contain `--work-tree` information with location of work tree. Which I am not sure how to work around both concisely and user-friendly.\r\n\r\nIf you have any actionable ideas, please feel free to share them here as comment (a bit too soon for the PR, I think).",
