        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Wow!\r\nLive and learn... every day?! I had *no idea* that the `.git` directory could be a *file* containing a `key: value` pair (which isn't even the syntax for `.gitconfig`) that specifies where the Git dir is.\r\nThank you, I'll check this out later today.\r\n\r\nI suspect it won't be most ideal for me, because I don't like the idea of always being inside a Git repo, with my shell prompt telling me what branch I'm on... But that's a solution. Although at this point, I might as well just have a regular Git directory at `$HOME` ;) Or am I missing something?\r\n\r\nRegarding the usage of `gen_source.git` I was envisioning, I figured I'd manually instruct `mini.diff` to switch sources when I want to, just like you could choose to go from Git to \"last saved version\". And yeah, I did think maybe some smart-ish hand-rolled AutoCmd switch might be helpful, but that just isn't generic/flexible enough to land in tool used by the community rather than just by me. Also, it gets wonky: what if we have two buffers, one to a \"regular Git\" file, and one to a \"global dotfile Git\" file?\r\n\r\nIn the end, do I really dislike knowing that I am inside a Git work tree all the time? Anyhow, thank you again for your investigation, I've got some thinking to do.\r\n\r\nCheers!",
