        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Live and learn... every day?! I had _no idea_ that the `.git` directory could be a _file_ containing a `key: value` pair (which isn't even the syntax for `.gitconfig`) that specifies where the Git dir is.\r\n> Thank you, I'll check this out later today.\r\n\r\nI suspect it won't work 100% (I tried): 'mini.diff' will recognize that it is \"inside\" git repository, but getting reference text would fail as it requires also having the `--work-tree` parameter. And that is the problem because I am not sure about the proper way of getting it automatically with the bare repo case. Because (as far as I understood) that's the point of bare repo: it just contains Git information which is not tied to the work tree (thus requiring user to explicitly say where the work-tree is).\r\n\r\nThe `--separate-git-dir` works as expected, though. This automatically creates '.git' file and sets `core.worktree` in the repo config to a proper path (expanded '~' in this case).\r\n\r\n> Although at this point, I might as well just have a regular Git directory at `$HOME` ;) Or am I missing something?\r\n\r\nHmm... That's a great point actually. The only benefit of `--separate-git-dir` is to have '.git' directory outside of work tree directory (for some reason).\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nAll that leads me to believe that having 'mini.diff' (and 'mini.git', for that matter) work with bare repos is a challenge that needs a *very* compelling reason or a *concise* eventual solution. If you or anybody else figure out either one (preferably the concise solution), please leave a comment here. I'll close this for now.",
